  "Question": "The area of a circle is doubled when its radius \\(r\\) is increased by \\(n.\\) Then \\(r\\) equals:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(n(\\sqrt2+1)\\)",
    "B": "\\(n(\\sqrt2-1)\\)",
    "C": "\\(n\\)",
    "D": "\\(n(2-\\sqrt2)\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{\\sqrt2+1}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "A circle's area is proportional to the square of its radius, so doubling the area gives\n\\[\n\\pi(r+n)^2=2\\pi r^2.\n\\]\nHere \\(r>0\\) and \\(n>0\\). Canceling \\(\\pi\\) and taking positive square roots, since both radii are positive, gives\n\\[\nr+n=\\sqrt{2}r,\n\\qquad n=(\\sqrt{2}-1)r.\n\\]\nBecause \\(\\sqrt{2}-1>0\\), we may divide by it. Multiplying numerator and denominator by \\(\\sqrt{2}+1\\) then gives\n\\[\nr=\\frac{n}{\\sqrt{2}-1}\n=\\frac{n(\\sqrt{2}+1)}{2-1}\n=n(\\sqrt{2}+1).\n\\]\nThus \\(r=n(\\sqrt{2}+1)\\), choice A.",
